<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1729">1729</a>–<a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Lessing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gotthold Ephraim Lessing (1729–1781) was a German dramatist, critic and philosopher of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg-dramaturgy">Hamburg Dramaturgy</a>, written for the city's short-lived National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in 1767–1769, laid foundations of German dramatic criticism, and his plays Minna von Barnhelm, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilia">Emilia</a> Galotti and Nathan the Wise became classics. Nathan the Wise is a celebrated plea for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gotthold-ephraim-lessing.jpg" alt="Gotthold Ephraim Lessing" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anna Rosina de Gasc · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gotthold_Ephraim_Lessing.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</b> wrote his Hamburg Dramaturgy for the city's short-lived National Theatre in 1767–1769, and Felix Mendelssohn and Johannes Brahms were born in Hamburg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In Germany <b>Gotthold Ephraim Lessing</b> pleaded for tolerance among Jews, Christians and Muslims in his play Nathan the Wise of 1779, and Kant, who taught at the University of Königsberg, examined the limits of reason itself.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Augustus the Younger built the Herzog August Library, one of the great libraries of Europe, where <b>Lessing</b> later served as librarian.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Principality of Wolfenbüttel</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamenz">Kamenz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Brunswick, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.27,14.1&amp;year=1762" aria-label="Kamenz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4870 388 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gotthold Ephraim Lessing"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5320 658h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5320 658h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.27,14.1&amp;year=1762">What was here in 1762? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If each of you Has had a ring presented by his father, Let each believe his own the real ring.&#8221;</p><footer>Nathan the Wise (1779), act 3, scene 7, the parable of the three rings &#183; tr. William Taylor of Norwich, 1791 &#183; spoken by Nathan, giving the judge's counsel in his parable &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3820/pg3820-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;How can I less believe in my forefathers Than thou in thine. How can I ask of thee To own that thy forefathers falsified In order to yield mine the praise of truth.&#8221;</p><footer>Nathan the Wise (1779), act 3, scene 7 &#183; tr. William Taylor of Norwich, 1791 &#183; spoken by Nathan to Saladin &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/3820/pg3820-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Education is Revelation coming to the Individual Man; and Revelation is Education which has come, and is yet coming, to the Human Race.&#8221;</p><footer>The Education of the Human Race (1780), section 2 &#183; tr. F. W. Robertson (as printed in the Harvard Classics, 1910) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/5637/pg5637-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
